= Four Substances Decoction (Sì Wù Tāng, 四物湯)

_The foundational blood-nourishing formula of Chinese medicine, and the deliberate counterpart of 四君子湯 — qi and blood, two base formulas built as a matched pair. Four drugs: *熟地黃* (prepared rehmannia), *當歸* (dang gui), *白芍* (white peony), and *川芎* (chuanxiong). It treats *血虛 (blood vacuity)*: a sallow or lustreless complexion, dizziness, palpitations with insomnia, dry skin and hair, numbness or tingling of the limbs, scanty or delayed menstruation, a pale tongue, and a thin (細) pulse. Its architecture is the lesson: two drugs supply blood and two keep that supply moving and un-stagnant, so the formula nourishes without becoming a static, cloying mass — "tonifying blood without stagnating blood." It is the parent of the whole of classical Chinese gynaecology's menstrual-regulation formulary, and the single most consequential thing to understand about it is that it is *cloying*, which makes misapplying it to a weak digestion the commonest error in this book's formula section._

== Name & Meaning

*四物湯* — *四* (sì) "four" + *物* (wù) + *湯* (tāng) "decoction." *物* is the plainest word in the medical lexicon: "thing, substance, material, item." Unlike 四君子湯, whose name carries a Confucian metaphor and a pun, *四物湯 is named with deliberate flatness* — "the four-things decoction." The contrast between the two base formulas' names is itself worth noticing: the qi formula gets a moral conceit, the blood formula gets a bare count.

*Standard English renderings:* "Four Substances Decoction," "Four Materials Decoction," "Four-Agents Decoction," "Four Things Decoction." In patent-medicine commerce it is often simply "Four Substances." *Japanese (Kampo):* 四物湯 *Shimotsutō*. *Korean:* 사물탕 *samultang*.

*No homonym trap* of the kind 四君子湯 carries — but note that *四物* in non-medical Chinese is an ordinary phrase ("four things") and is not a fixed cultural term; searching it outside a medical context returns nothing relevant.

== Source & History

The standard attribution is to the *太平惠民和劑局方 (Tài Píng Huì Mín Hé Jì Jú Fāng)*, the Song imperial formulary (1107–1110) — the same source as 四君子湯, which is why the two are so often presented as a matched pair, and which this book cites consistently across entries (see `substances/dang-gui.typ`, `substances/rehmannia.typ`).

*But the formula is older than its canonical source, and its original purpose was different.* It is widely traced to the Tang-period *仙授理傷續斷秘方 (Xiān Shòu Lǐ Shāng Xù Duàn Mì Fāng,* "Secret Formulas Received from Immortals to Treat Wounds and Reconnect Fractures"), attributed to *藺道人 (Lin Daoren)* and conventionally dated to the mid-ninth century — a *trauma and orthopaedics* text, in which the combination serves to move and replenish blood after injury. The Hejijufang is where it is recast as the canonical *blood-nourishing and menstruation-regulating* formula, and that is the identity it has carried ever since. *The repurposing is the interesting part of its history*: a formula devised for the stagnant, damaged blood of trauma became the base formula for blood vacuity, which is a different pathology — and the residue of the original use is still visible in the formula's unusual composition, since a purely nourishing formula would not need 川芎 at all. *Flagged:* the Tang attribution is standard in the modern literature but the textual transmission of that work is complicated, and the dating should be verified against a specific edition rather than taken from secondary sources.#footnote[_Tài Píng Huì Mín Hé Jì Jú Fāng_ (太平惠民和劑局方), 1107–1110, for the canonical blood-nourishing formula. The earlier appearance in _Xiān Shòu Lǐ Shāng Xù Duàn Mì Fāng_ (仙授理傷續斷秘方), attributed to Lin Daoren, Tang period (conventionally mid-9th c.), is standard in the modern formula literature — Bensky & Barolet, _Formulas & Strategies_, 2nd ed., notes the trauma-formula origin. The transmission history of the Tang text is complicated and the dating is flagged for verification against a specific edition.]

*A composition divergence in this book, flagged rather than smoothed:* `substances/rehmannia.typ` describes 四物湯 as using *生地黃* (raw rehmannia) "or 熟地黃 in tonifying-blood variants," while `substances/dang-gui.typ` gives *熟地黃* flatly. The standard transmitted Hejijufang composition uses the *prepared* root (熟乾地黃), and that is what this entry treats as the base formula; the *生地黃* substitution is a real and much-used variant that *changes the formula's character* — see Modifications. The two substance entries differ in emphasis rather than in fact, but the rehmannia entry's phrasing is the looser of the two and would be worth tightening.

*Its standing:* with 四君子湯, one of the two 基礎方 (base formulas) of the whole tonifying formulary, and the parent of classical Chinese gynaecology. 桃紅四物湯, 膠艾湯, 八珍湯, 十全大補湯, 溫經湯, and a large part of the menstrual-regulation literature descend from or incorporate it.

== Composition

*Classical proportions (Hejijufang):* the four drugs in *equal parts* — *各等分* — ground to a coarse powder, *3 錢 (qián)* per dose decocted in water. As with 四君子湯, the equal-parts instruction is the classical form; modern practice has differentiated the doses considerably.

*Modern clinical decoction doses* (reference figures requiring verification against current pharmacopoeial and clinical practice):
- *熟地黃 (shú dì huáng,* prepared rehmannia) — *12–15 g* (the substance entry gives a 9–30 g range for the single drug).
- *當歸 (dāng guī,* Chinese angelica root) — *9–12 g*.
- *白芍 (bái sháo,* white peony root) — *9–12 g*.
- *川芎 (chuān xiōng,* Ligusticum rhizome) — *6–8 g* — *deliberately the smallest dose in the formula*, for reasons given below.

*On proportions:* the modern reduction of 川芎 relative to the other three is not arbitrary. It is the only frankly moving, ascending, acrid drug in a formula whose purpose is to nourish; at equal parts it can overwhelm the formula's tonifying intent, and in a blood-vacuous patient an excess of it produces headache and agitation. The classical equal-parts instruction should be read as a *Song powder-dose convention* rather than as a claim that all four contribute equally.

*Processing matters here as much as in 四君子湯:* *熟地黃* (steam-prepared) and *生地黃* (raw) are *different drugs with opposite thermal properties* — see the ingredient block below and `substances/rehmannia.typ`. Likewise *白芍* (white peony, nourishing and astringing) and *赤芍* (red peony, cooling and blood-moving) are distinct drugs, not grades.

=== 熟地黃 (shú dì huáng, prepared rehmannia) — 君, sovereign

*Properties:* Sweet (甘); *slightly warm* (微溫). *Channels:* Liver (肝), Kidney (腎). *Principal actions:* 補血滋陰 (tonifies blood, enriches yin), 益精填髓 (augments essence, fills the marrow).

*Function in this formula:* it is the *substantial supply of blood* — the heavy, sweet, moistening drug that actually replenishes the deficient substance. Nothing else in the formula does this; the other three act on blood that 熟地黃 provides or that the patient already has.

*Why the prepared root, not the raw:* this is the formula's single most consequential composition decision. *生地黃* (raw) is *cold*, sweet-bitter, enters Heart–Liver–Kidney, and *cools blood and clears heat*; the steam-preparation (九蒸九晒, nine steamings and nine sun-dryings) transforms it into a *slightly warm*, more purely sweet drug that *tonifies* blood and essence, with the Heart channel dropped and the Kidney channel deepened. A blood-*vacuity* formula needs the warming, supplementing form. Substituting the raw root produces a *blood-cooling* formula for heat in the blood — a legitimate and named variant, but a different prescription.

*The liability it brings:* 熟地黃 is *滋膩 (zīnì,* rich and cloying) — sticky, heavy, and hard for a weak digestion to handle. `substances/rehmannia.typ` flags exactly this, and notes the classical correction of pairing it with *砂仁* or ginger. This single property is the origin of most of the misapplication problems treated below.

See `substances/rehmannia.typ` for the full treatment, including the 生/熟 drug-form system.

=== 當歸 (dāng guī, Chinese angelica root) — 臣, minister

*Properties:* Sweet (甘), pungent (辛), slightly bitter (苦); warm (溫). *Channels:* Liver (肝), Heart (心), Spleen (脾). *Principal actions:* 補血 (tonifies blood), 活血 (invigorates blood), 調經止痛 (regulates menstruation, stops pain), 潤腸通便 (moistens the intestines).

*Function in this formula:* 當歸 is the formula's *hinge*, because it is the one drug that *both* tonifies and moves blood. It reinforces the sovereign's supplementation while simultaneously ensuring the new blood circulates rather than sitting; and its Liver and Heart channel entries connect the formula to the two organs that store and govern blood. In gynaecological use it is also the drug that makes the formula *regulate menstruation* rather than merely nourish.

*Why this drug rather than its neighbours:* no other common blood tonic is simultaneously a blood mover. 白芍 nourishes but astringes; 熟地黃 nourishes but is static; 阿膠 (donkey-hide gelatin) nourishes more richly but is even more cloying and does not move. Remove 當歸 and the formula loses the property that makes it a *regulating* formula and not merely a supplementing one.

See `substances/dang-gui.typ` for the full treatment, including the 當歸頭/身/尾 part-distinctions, which matter here: the *body* (當歸身) is preferred where tonification dominates and the *tail* (當歸尾) where movement does.

=== 白芍 (bái sháo, white peony root) — 佐, assistant

*Properties:* Bitter (苦), sour (酸); slightly cold (微寒). *Channels:* Liver (肝), Spleen (脾). *Principal actions:* 養血斂陰 (nourishes blood, astringes yin), 柔肝止痛 (softens the Liver, relieves pain), 平抑肝陽 (calms ascendant Liver yang).

*Function in this formula:* two jobs, both corrective. It *nourishes blood and yin* alongside the sovereign — but it does so while *astringing*, which is the counterweight to 當歸's and 川芎's outward movement, so that the formula does not disperse the very blood it is building. And it *softens the Liver* (柔肝): blood vacuity characteristically leaves the Liver dry and tense, producing cramping pain, irritability, and — in gynaecology — dysmenorrhoea; 白芍 addresses that directly, and is the drug behind the formula's pain-relieving reputation.

Its *slightly cold* property also moderates the warmth of the other three, keeping a formula of three warm drugs from becoming frankly heating.

*Why white peony, not red:* *赤芍 (chì sháo,* red peony) is *cooling and blood-moving*, with no nourishing or astringing action. Substituting it converts the formula toward clearing heat and breaking stasis — again a real variant, not an equivalent. *White nourishes and holds; red cools and moves.*

_No substance entry yet for 白芍; the summary above is given in full for that reason, and the glossary carries the term._

=== 川芎 (chuān xiōng, Ligusticum rhizome) — 使, envoy

*Properties:* Pungent (辛); warm (溫). *Channels:* Liver (肝), Gallbladder (膽), Pericardium (心包). *Principal actions:* 活血行氣 (invigorates blood and moves qi), 祛風止痛 (dispels wind, relieves pain).

*Function in this formula — and the reason the formula is not merely a pile of tonics:* 川芎 is classically called *血中之氣藥 (xuè zhōng zhī qì yào,* "the qi-medicine within the blood") — it moves *qi* within the *blood* aspect. Blood does not circulate by itself; it is moved by qi. A formula of three rich, sweet, substantial drugs would otherwise produce a static mass that burdens the middle, and 川芎's acrid, ascending, dispersing action is what keeps the whole prescription *moving*. It is the smallest dose and the most indispensable idea in the composition.

It also carries the formula *upward and outward* — to the head and the exterior — which is why 四物湯-based formulas treat blood-vacuity headache and dizziness, and why the formula appears in dermatological prescriptions for dry, itchy skin ("treat wind by first treating blood; when blood moves, wind is extinguished" — *治風先治血，血行風自滅*).

*Why this drug:* nothing else in the common materia medica performs the qi-within-blood function at a dose small enough not to overwhelm a tonifying formula. *Its liability:* being acrid, warm and ascending, it is the ingredient most likely to cause trouble when the pattern is wrong — see Misapplication.

_No substance entry yet for 川芎; the summary above is given in full for that reason, and the glossary carries the term._

== Formula Architecture

The pattern is *血虛 (blood vacuity)*. In this system blood is stored by the *Liver*, governed by the *Heart*, and produced from food by the *Spleen*; it moistens and anchors. Its insufficiency therefore shows in three registers at once: *the tissues it fails to moisten* (dry skin and hair, lustreless complexion, brittle nails), *the mind it fails to anchor* (palpitations, insomnia, poor memory, anxiety), and *the periphery it fails to reach* (numbness, tingling, blurred vision, floaters) — plus, in women, *scanty or delayed menstruation*, since menstrual blood is the most visible index of blood sufficiency.

The formula's design answers a problem specific to supplementing blood: *blood is a substance, and substantial tonics are heavy*. Three of the four drugs are rich, sweet and moistening; left to themselves they would form a cloying, static mass that a blood-vacuous patient — whose Spleen is usually also weak, since the Spleen produces blood — cannot digest. So the composition is deliberately split:

- *Supplying blood:* 熟地黃 (the substance) and 當歸 (substance plus movement).
- *Keeping it moving:* 當歸 again, and 川芎 (qi within blood).
- *Keeping it held:* 白芍, which astringes and prevents the movers from dispersing what has been built, and softens the Liver that blood vacuity has left tense.

The maxim the tradition draws from this is *補血而不滯血 — "tonify blood without stagnating blood."* Two tonics and two regulators, one of which (當歸) does both jobs at once. The *四君子湯* parallel is exact in form and different in content: there, supplementation is paired with *drainage* (補中有瀉) because the Spleen generates damp; here, supplementation is paired with *movement* because blood tonics are static. Both base formulas say the same structural thing — *a supplementing formula must build in its own corrective* — about two different organs.

*What breaks if an ingredient is removed:*
- *Remove 川芎* and the formula becomes purely nourishing and markedly more cloying — usable in a robust digestion, burdensome otherwise, and no longer a *regulating* formula.
- *Remove 當歸* and it loses the tonify-and-move hinge, and with it most of its gynaecological application.
- *Remove 白芍* and the two movers are unopposed: the formula disperses rather than accumulates, and loses its pain-relieving and Liver-softening action.
- *Remove 熟地黃* and there is nothing left to supply blood — the remainder is a mild blood-moving prescription, not a tonic.

*The qi-and-blood pair.* 四物湯 and 四君子湯 are built as counterparts and are routinely combined into *八珍湯 (Bā Zhēn Tāng,* Eight Treasures) for dual vacuity. The clinical reason the combination is so common is worth stating: *qi generates blood*, so sustained blood vacuity almost always has a qi-vacuity component, and treating blood alone in a patient with a weak Spleen both fails to address the cause and feeds the cloying problem. In practice, 八珍湯 is prescribed more often than either base formula alone.#footnote[Pattern mechanism, the 補血而不滯血 reading, the 血中之氣藥 epithet for 川芎, and the 治風先治血 maxim: Bensky & Barolet, _Formulas & Strategies_, 2nd ed.; standard modern formula-theory teaching texts. The "remove an ingredient" analysis is the conventional pedagogical treatment of this formula.]

== Indications & Pattern

*Pattern:* *血虛 (blood vacuity)*, and in gynaecology *血虛* with menstrual irregularity — classically *營血虛滯* (constructive-blood vacuity with stagnation), which names both halves of what the formula addresses.

*Presenting signs:*
- *面色萎黃 / 蒼白無華* — a sallow-yellow or pale, *lustreless* complexion (the lack of lustre is the blood sign, as distinct from the plain pallor of qi vacuity).
- *頭暈目眩* — dizziness and visual dizziness; *目澀 / 視物昏花* — dry eyes, blurred vision, floaters.
- *心悸失眠* — palpitations with insomnia (blood failing to anchor the spirit).
- *手足麻木* — numbness or tingling of the extremities.
- *皮膚乾燥, 爪甲不榮, 髮枯* — dry skin, brittle lustreless nails, dry hair.
- *月經量少 / 後期 / 閉經* — scanty, delayed, or absent menstruation; dull lower-abdominal pain *relieved by pressure*; pale, thin menstrual blood.
- *Tongue:* pale, *thin and often dry*, with little coat (舌淡). *Pulse:* *thin/fine* (細), often also thready-weak (細弱) or wiry if the Liver is involved.

*Modern correlates — correlations, not equivalences:* iron-deficiency and other anaemias, functional menstrual disorders (oligomenorrhoea, amenorrhoea, dysmenorrhoea), postpartum and post-haemorrhagic recovery, chronic dry skin conditions and some eczema presentations, and convalescent states. *These are contexts in which the pattern is often found, not diagnoses the formula treats* — and the anaemia correlation in particular is a trap, treated below.

*Differentiation from adjacent formulas* — where the pattern has been correctly identified:
- *四君子湯* — the *qi* counterpart; for fatigue, poor appetite, loose stools, weak voice, with a pale *swollen* tongue. See `formulas/four-gentlemen-decoction.typ`.
- *八珍湯* — both patterns together; by far the commonest real-world choice.
- *當歸補血湯* — Li Dongyuan's two-drug formula (黃芪 5 : 當歸 1) for *blood vacuity with marked heat signs from vacuity*, a quite different mechanism (qi generating blood) despite the overlapping name.
- *桃紅四物湯* — where frank *stasis* accompanies the vacuity (clots, fixed stabbing pain).
- *膠艾湯 (芎歸膠艾湯)* — where blood vacuity presents with *bleeding* — uterine bleeding, threatened miscarriage; adds 阿膠 and 艾葉 to hold blood in.
- *歸脾湯* — blood vacuity with *Heart-Spleen* involvement dominating: anxiety, overthinking, poor appetite, and bleeding from failure to contain. See `substances/fuling.typ`.

== Misapplication & Look-Alike Patterns

_The classical literature is generous about what a formula treats and terse about what happens when it is given wrongly. For 四物湯 the consequences are unusually predictable, because they follow directly from two properties of the composition: it is *cloying* (three rich drugs), and it *moves blood* (two acrid ones). Nearly every misapplication is one of those two properties meeting a patient who cannot tolerate it. Mechanisms below are clinical commonplaces in the Chinese-medical literature except where flagged as this book's inference._

=== If given to the wrong pattern

- *Spleen vacuity with weak digestion (脾虛) — the commonest error, and the one to expect.* *Mechanism:* 熟地黃 is 滋膩, sticky and heavy; a Spleen that is already failing to transform food cannot transform a rich tonic either, and the formula sits in the middle burner undigested. *Consequence:* abdominal fullness and distension, loss of appetite, nausea, loose stools, and a *thick greasy tongue coat* appearing where there was none — often within a few days. The classical phrase is *滋膩礙胃*, "cloying and obstructing the stomach." *Correction:* add aromatic qi-movers (*砂仁*, *陳皮*) as `substances/rehmannia.typ` describes for 熟地黃 generally; or switch to *八珍湯*, treating the Spleen at the same time; or treat the Spleen *first* and the blood after. In a markedly weak digestion the order matters — there is a standing clinical maxim that one cannot build blood through a Spleen that cannot work.
- *Damp or phlegm patterns (濕困 / 痰濕).* *Mechanism:* the same cloying property, meeting a middle burner already encumbered. *Consequence:* heaviness, chest and epigastric oppression, nausea, a thickening greasy coat; the damp pattern worsens measurably while the blood pattern does not improve, because production is blocked. *Correction:* resolve the damp first (平胃散, 二陳湯 register); 四物湯 is not a formula to give through a greasy tongue coat.
- *Heat in the blood, or active bleeding (血熱 / 出血).* *Mechanism:* 當歸 and especially 川芎 are warm, acrid and *moving*; blood that is already moving too forcefully, or hot, is driven further. *Consequence:* increased bleeding — heavier menstrual flow, prolonged periods, epistaxis — and in heat patterns, aggravated restlessness and a sense of heat rising. *Correction:* this is the case for the *生地黃 + 赤芍* variant (cooling, not warming), or for a bleeding-specific formula such as *膠艾湯*. Giving the standard warm base formula to frank blood heat is actively counterproductive, not merely ineffective.
- *Yin vacuity with vigorous fire (陰虛火旺).* *Mechanism:* 川芎's acrid-warm-*ascending* action, in a patient whose problem is unanchored heat rising. *Consequence:* headache, facial flushing, irritability, worsened night sweats and insomnia — typically attributable to the 川芎 specifically, which is why reducing or removing it often resolves the reaction while the rest of the formula remains tolerable. *Correction:* reduce or omit 川芎; consider a yin-tonifying formula instead (`substances/rehmannia.typ`, the 六味地黃丸 register).
- *Acute exterior illness (表證未解).* *Mechanism:* the standing rule against tonifying while a pathogen is unresolved — *閉門留寇*, "shutting the door and leaving the bandit inside." *Consequence:* a protracted illness, with the fever or cough dragging on rather than resolving. *Correction:* release the exterior first; tonify afterwards.
- *Heavy menstrual bleeding, given during the period.* *Mechanism:* both movers acting at precisely the point in the cycle when flow is already at maximum. *Consequence:* increased volume and duration. *Correction:* standard practice is to time 四物湯-based prescriptions to the *inter-menstrual* phase, or to use a holding variant (膠艾湯) during flow. *This timing rule is a clinical commonplace in Chinese gynaecology and is worth stating explicitly*, since it is a dosing-schedule error rather than a pattern error and so falls outside the usual contraindication lists.
- *Pregnancy.* *Mechanism:* 川芎 moves blood. *Consequence/status:* 四物湯 *derivatives* are genuinely used in pregnancy — *膠艾湯* is a classical formula for threatened miscarriage — but the base formula at full 川芎 dose is cautioned. *Correction:* this is a practitioner's decision, not a rule this entry can state; see Cautions.

=== Patterns mistaken for this one

- *Qi vacuity mistaken for blood vacuity — the most frequent confusion.* *Why it resembles:* both give fatigue, pallor, dizziness, and a pale tongue. *Distinguishing signs:* qi vacuity has *shortness of breath, a weak low voice, spontaneous sweating*, and a pale *swollen* tongue with *teeth-marks*, pulse weak (虛弱); blood vacuity has a *lustreless* complexion, *dry* skin and hair, *numbness*, *palpitations with insomnia*, *scanty menses*, a pale *thin and dry* tongue, pulse *thin* (細). *The deciding sign in practice is the tongue body:* swollen with teeth-marks → qi; thin and dry → blood. *What to give instead:* 四君子湯 — or, very often, 八珍湯 for both.
- *Blood stasis mistaken for blood vacuity.* *Why it resembles:* both present with menstrual irregularity and lower-abdominal pain, and both can show a dull complexion. *Distinguishing signs:* stasis gives *fixed, stabbing* pain that is *worse with pressure*, *dark clots*, a *purple or spotted* tongue, and a *choppy* (澀) pulse; vacuity gives *dull* pain *better with pressure*, *pale thin* flow, pale tongue, thin pulse. *Consequence of the error:* 四物湯 given to frank stasis is too nourishing and not nearly moving enough — the cloying drugs may thicken the picture while the stasis is untouched. *What to give instead:* *桃紅四物湯* at minimum, or a dedicated stasis formula (血府逐瘀湯).
- *Yin vacuity mistaken for blood vacuity.* *Why it resembles:* the two overlap genuinely — both are "insufficiency of substance," both give dryness, dizziness, insomnia, and a thin pulse, and blood vacuity can progress into yin vacuity. *Distinguishing signs:* yin vacuity adds *heat*: night sweats, five-centre heat (五心煩熱), afternoon flushing, a *red* tongue with *little or no coat*, and a thin *rapid* (細數) pulse. Blood vacuity's tongue is *pale*, not red. *Consequence of the error:* the warm 當歸 and ascending 川芎 aggravate the empty heat. *What to give instead:* a yin tonic (六味地黃丸 register), or 四物湯 with 生地黃 substituted and 川芎 reduced.
- *Damp-heat or food stagnation presenting as fatigue and poor complexion.* *Why it resembles:* the patient is tired and looks unwell, which invites a reflexive tonic. *Distinguishing sign:* the *tongue coat* — thick, greasy, often yellow. Blood vacuity has *little* coat. *Consequence of the error:* straightforward aggravation, per the damp case above. *What to give instead:* clear and resolve first; tonify later.
- *Liver blood vacuity vs. Liver qi constraint.* *Why it resembles:* both give irritability, menstrual irregularity, and premenstrual tension. *Distinguishing signs:* constraint gives *rib-side distension*, sighing, symptoms that fluctuate with mood and stress, and a *wiry* (弦) pulse; blood vacuity gives the dryness-and-anchoring signs and a *thin* pulse. *What to give instead:* 逍遙散 — which, notably, contains both 當歸 and 白芍, i.e. half of this formula, precisely because the two patterns so often coexist.
- *The modern trap: anaemia is not 血虛, and 血虛 is not anaemia.* The two correlate loosely and are routinely equated, in both directions, by practitioners and patients. *They are not the same category:* a patient with laboratory iron-deficiency anaemia may present with a *damp-heat* or *qi-vacuity* pattern and do badly on 四物湯; and a patient with entirely normal haemoglobin may present with textbook 血虛 — lustreless complexion, numbness, scanty menses, thin pulse — and respond well. *Practical consequence:* a haemoglobin value is not a prescription, and *if the pattern and the blood count disagree, the formula follows the pattern*. Conversely, a genuine iron-deficiency anaemia has a cause that needs investigating, and a blood-nourishing formula is not a substitute for finding it — this is the point at which Chinese-medical pattern reasoning should defer to biomedical workup rather than compete with it.#footnote[Misapplication consequences are clinical commonplaces in the Chinese formula literature: the 滋膩礙胃 cloying problem of 熟地黃 (cf. `substances/rehmannia.typ`, which flags the same caution for the single drug and gives the 砂仁/ginger correction), the bleeding risk of warm blood-movers, the 閉門留寇 rule against tonifying an unresolved exterior, and the inter-menstrual timing convention of Chinese gynaecology. Bensky & Barolet, _Formulas & Strategies_, 2nd ed.; standard modern teaching texts. The anaemia-vs-血虛 discussion is this book's own framing of a well-recognized problem, and is stated as a reasoning principle rather than as a claim from any classical source.]

== Modifications & Derived Formulas

*Named derived formulas:*
- *桃紅四物湯 (Táo Hóng Sì Wù Tāng)* — *+ 桃仁 + 紅花*. Blood vacuity *with stasis*: clotted, painful, delayed menstruation. The standard first step when stasis signs appear. See `substances/dang-gui.typ`.
- *膠艾湯 / 芎歸膠艾湯 (Jiāo Ài Tāng)* — *+ 阿膠 + 艾葉 + 甘草*, from the *金匱要略*. Blood vacuity *with bleeding* — uterine bleeding, bleeding in pregnancy, threatened miscarriage. Converts the formula from moving to *holding*; the classical answer to the bleeding contraindication above.
- *八珍湯 (Bā Zhēn Tāng)* — *+ 四君子湯*. Dual qi-and-blood vacuity; the commonest real-world prescription of this family. See `formulas/four-gentlemen-decoction.typ`.
- *十全大補湯 (Shí Quán Dà Bǔ Tāng)* — *八珍湯 + 黃芪 + 肉桂*. Deeper dual vacuity with cold. See `substances/astragalus.typ`.
- *人參養榮湯* — a further expansion for qi-blood-yin vacuity with consumption. See `substances/schisandra.typ`.
- *聖愈湯 (Shèng Yù Tāng)* — *+ 人參 + 黃芪*; blood vacuity with marked qi vacuity and bleeding tendency.
- *溫經湯 (Wēn Jīng Tāng)* — incorporates the 四物 drugs within a warming, stasis-dispelling gynaecological formula for cold in the vessels with irregular menstruation.
- *玉燭散*, *四物消風飲* — purgative and dermatological extensions respectively; the latter applies the *治風先治血* principle to itchy skin disease.

*Common modifications (加減):*
- *生地黃 for 熟地黃, 赤芍 for 白芍* — converts the formula from *nourishing-and-warming* to *cooling-and-moving*, for blood heat. The single most important variant, and effectively a different prescription.
- *+ 阿膠, 艾葉* — to hold blood (toward 膠艾湯).
- *+ 黃芪, 人參* — for concurrent qi vacuity (toward 八珍湯 / 聖愈湯).
- *+ 砂仁, 陳皮* — to counteract the cloying property in a weak digestion; the standard correction.
- *Reduce or omit 川芎* — in yin vacuity with rising heat, in headache-prone patients, and in pregnancy.
- *+ 香附, 延胡索* — for dysmenorrhoea with qi stagnation.

*Kampo note:* 四物湯 (*Shimotsutō*) is a standard Kampo prescription, and the related *当帰芍薬散 (Tōki-shakuyaku-san)* — which shares 當歸, 白芍 and 川芎 but adds damp-draining drugs — is among the most prescribed Kampo gynaecological formulas in Japan, with a clinical literature of its own.#footnote[Derived formulas and modifications: Bensky & Barolet, _Formulas & Strategies_, 2nd ed.; _Jīn Guì Yào Lüè_ (金匱要略) for 膠艾湯. Kampo prescribing patterns for 当帰芍薬散: Japanese Kampo clinical literature — verify current prescribing statistics.]

== Preparation & Administration

*Classical method (Hejijufang).* The four drugs in equal parts, ground to a coarse powder; *3 錢* per dose decocted in roughly a cupful of water, reduced, and drunk warm before food. The 錢 is approximately *3.7 g* in late-imperial measure, so a dose is on the order of *11 g* of combined powder. *The exact wording should be verified against a specific edition* rather than taken from this entry.

*Modern decoction — the usual form:*
1. Combine at the modern doses above.
2. *Soak* in cold water 20–30 minutes.
3. Bring to a boil, then simmer; two successive decoctions, liquids combined.
4. Divide into *two doses daily*, taken *warm*.

*熟地黃 needs the long decoction* — it is dense and its sweet, substantial fraction extracts slowly, so it is not a candidate for short boiling. *川芎 is aromatic* and its volatile fraction is driven off by prolonged hard boiling; where a practitioner wants its moving action preserved it may be added in the later part of the decoction (the *後下* principle), though in this formula it is more commonly decocted with the rest.

*Timing — and here the formula has a convention of its own.* As a tonic it is classically taken *before food* or between meals. But in gynaecological use the more consequential timing is *within the menstrual cycle*: 四物湯-based prescriptions are conventionally given in the *follicular / inter-menstrual* phase to build blood, and withheld or swapped for a holding variant during heavy flow (see Misapplication). Chinese gynaecology's cycle-phase prescribing is built on this base formula, and the timing is part of the prescription rather than an optional refinement.

*Course.* Cumulative, over weeks to months; blood is built slowly. Menstrual-regulation courses are conventionally reckoned in *cycles*, not days.

*Modern prepared forms.* Granules, pills, and patents are widely available; *四物* is also sold as a consumer tonic in Taiwan and Hong Kong, notably as a packaged drink taken after menstruation — a popular-culture use well outside the pattern-matched prescription, and worth distinguishing from clinical use. Granule concentration ratios vary by manufacturer and are *not interchangeable gram-for-gram* with raw-herb decoction.#footnote[Preparation, the 錢 conversion (~3.7 g, with period and regional variation), and modern decoction practice: Bensky & Barolet, _Formulas & Strategies_, 2nd ed.; standard Chinese pharmacy references. The classical Hejijufang instruction is paraphrased as to its shape and flagged for verification against a specific edition. Cycle-phase prescribing is standard in modern Chinese gynaecology. The consumer-tonic register in Taiwan and Hong Kong is a commercial/popular use, flagged as distinct from clinical prescription.]

== Cautions & Contraindications

_Pattern-matching errors and their consequences are treated above, under Misapplication. This section covers pharmacological safety._

*Pattern contraindications (summary; see Misapplication for mechanisms):* Spleen vacuity with weak digestion, damp and phlegm patterns, blood heat and active bleeding, yin vacuity with vigorous fire, acute exterior illness.

*Ingredient-derived cautions:*
- *當歸 — anticoagulant interaction.* Dang gui contains coumarin-class constituents and has reported antiplatelet activity; *case reports of potentiated warfarin effect with raised INR and bleeding* exist. Patients on warfarin or other anticoagulants should not start this formula without monitoring. See `substances/dang-gui.typ`.
- *當歸 — photosensitivity.* Furanocoumarins in Apiaceae roots can cause photosensitization at high or prolonged intake; a minor but real caution.
- *當歸 — laxative effect.* Its 潤腸 action loosens stools; in a patient already prone to loose stools this compounds the Spleen-vacuity problem described above.
- *川芎 — headache and agitation at high dose*, and the ascending action discussed under Misapplication; reduce in headache-prone and hypertensive patients.
- *熟地黃 — digestive burden* rather than toxicity; the limiting factor on dose and duration, and the reason for the 砂仁 correction.
- *白芍 — classically 反藜蘆* (antagonistic to _Veratrum_, among the *十八反*); of limited modern practical relevance, since _Veratrum_ is rarely prescribed, but it is the canonical incompatibility attached to this ingredient.

*Pregnancy.* The base formula at full 川芎 dose is cautioned; derivatives (膠艾湯) are classically used *in* pregnancy for bleeding and threatened miscarriage. The classical and modern positions do not align neatly, and this is a decision for a qualified practitioner rather than a rule stated here.

*Bleeding disorders and perioperative use.* Given the 當歸 antiplatelet signal and the formula's blood-moving action, discontinuation before surgery and caution in bleeding disorders are prudent — *a precaution by mechanism rather than a documented formula-level finding*, flagged as such.

*Reference information, not personalized medical advice.*#footnote[當歸 anticoagulant interaction and photosensitivity: see `substances/dang-gui.typ` and current herb-drug interaction literature; the warfarin case reports are documented though the formula-level risk is not quantified. 白芍 among the 十八反 (諸參辛芍叛藜蘆): standard Bencao-and-formula literature; cf. the 十八反 glossary entry. The perioperative precaution is explicitly an inference from mechanism, not a trial finding.]

== Modern Pharmacology

*The evidence situation, stated first:* formula-level research on 四物湯 is *thin relative to its clinical prominence*, predominantly Chinese- and Japanese-language, and mostly preclinical. The constituent herbs — especially 當歸 — have far larger literatures that *are not evidence for the compound*.

- *Haematopoietic effects* — the most-studied claim, and the one most directly matching the classical indication: animal models of induced anaemia and myelosuppression report promotion of erythropoiesis and recovery of blood counts, with effects attributed variously to the polysaccharide and ferulic-acid fractions. *Preclinical; the human evidence is limited and small-scale.*
- *Chemotherapy- and radiation-induced myelosuppression* — small clinical studies, largely Chinese, report supportive benefit; methodologically heterogeneous and not independently replicated at scale.
- *Gynaecological endpoints* — dysmenorrhoea and menstrual regulation have some small-trial support, but the better-evidenced gynaecological Kampo formula in this family is *当帰芍薬散 (Tōki-shakuyaku-san)* rather than 四物湯 itself; do not transfer its results across.
- *Oestrogenic activity* — in vitro and animal findings of weak oestrogen-receptor-mediated activity have been reported for 當歸 and for the formula. *Clinical significance is unestablished*, and this is the basis for a theoretical caution in hormone-sensitive conditions that is frequently overstated in popular sources in both directions.
- *Anti-inflammatory, antioxidant, dermatological* — preclinical signals consistent with the formula's use in dry skin conditions; preliminary.
- *What has not been shown:* that the four-drug combination outperforms its components, which is the specific claim the architecture makes; and that the formula corrects iron-deficiency anaemia as such — relevant given how often it is marketed for exactly that.#footnote[Haematopoietic and myelosuppression findings for 四物湯: predominantly Chinese- and Japanese-language preclinical literature plus small clinical studies — verify against current systematic reviews; evidence tier is preclinical-to-preliminary. Tōki-shakuyaku-san clinical literature is separate and should not be read across to 四物湯. Oestrogenic activity: in vitro and animal reports; clinical significance unestablished. The absence of combination-versus-components testing and of evidence for correcting iron-deficiency anaemia are stated as absences, per this book's standard on formula-level evidence.]

